A||||10||Mark||Engine Blower Leaking Water!||||1||
Z||000000||Mark||10-22-2001||10:58 AM||||I found the source of a water leak in my trunk ('86 GT).  After pouring about a gallon of water on and over the passanger-side trunk corner, water began dripping out of the engine blower housing (between the motor flange and the plastic housing). How is water getting into the blower housing?  How is the blower removed?  I've checked Chiltons and the search function.  No dice.<BR>Please help!||||reg||1
Z||000001||Voytek||10-22-2001||05:16 PM||||I've had a problem with a leak in the same area and on the left hand side.  My car is an '87 GT with the square gasket (other than the gasket being round or square, I don't know if the trunk finish varies from year to year or not).<P>This was the problem:<BR>Up until the leak, I always thought that the trunk was a metal enclosure, all around.  It turns out it's not.  Above the right (and left) wall of the trunk, the top is PLASTIC.  It is sealed to the metal (normally).  With time, the seal broke and I started to leak water right into the blower area.  It appeared that the water was properly draining around the trunk but it wasn't.  It took me FOREVER to figure out where it leaked.<P>When you remove the trunk liner, go over that seal very carefully.  Even if it looks good, I would re-seal it again.  I used a tube with this rubbery compound called Make-A-Gasket.  You should seal it in the forward and rear right corners just to be safe.<P>If this isn't your problem, good luck.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mark||10-22-2001||06:19 PM||||Voytek,  <BR>I removed the inside window trim and those plastic end pieces that make the trunk opening square.  Cleaned and re-sealed the plastic "corners" to the steel trunk frame, then reglued the trunk weatherstrip back down.  Before putting the window trim back on I decided to "give it the leak test".  I poured water on both sides (in, on and around the plastic corners), then checked for leaks.  Both sides initially looked fine, then after a few minutes a small amount of water driped from the blower.  After loosening the blower motor from the housing, more water poured out.  The bottom line is the blower somehow is taking on water.  My questions are: 1) how did the water get in the blower?  Did I just flood it excessively with the "leak test"? 2) How does the blower come out? I would like to pull it out just to clean/inspect everything and dry it out.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mark||10-22-2001||08:51 PM||||Surely someone has pulled one of these blowers before.  Can the blower be removed from the inside of the trunk (only)?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Mark||10-23-2001||05:49 PM||||Aw c'mon folks.  What's the secret to removing the blower?  Would it help if I said I wanted to remove the blower to install a 12 cyl Allison?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Mark||10-23-2001||09:32 PM||||Doesn't anyone have a Helm's manual?<P>What if I said I was a 19 yr old girl??  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000006||maryjane||10-23-2001||10:49 PM||||A 19 yr old girl named Mark?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_brow.gif"> This will bump it up one more time. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||10-23-2001||11:30 PM||||This is just a wild guess as I've never done this before <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif">, but what I would do is:<BR>remove the trunk carpet from that end, look for some bolts that appear to be holding te fan unit in place, and once they are removed see if you can remove the unit. As I said, I've never looked at my setup before, but really, how hard could it be. Did you even bother to look yet?||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mark||10-24-2001||05:37 PM||||Of course I've done all the obvious stuff!  Trust me, after removing the two nuts on top, the blower will not come out.   There must be another hidden fastener on the bottom somewhere (but I certainly can't see or feel it) or maybe the blower inlet is somehow attached to the trunk sheetmetal from the outside. It would probably be pretty obvious how the blower comes out if I would remove the rear clip. But you see, that's what I want to avoid doing!  This thing may be kindof like interior trim.  Sometimes you just have to pull harder...but you better be damn sure you got all the screws first! ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Mark||10-24-2001||06:01 PM||||OK.  Here's the deal.  The blower is held to the trunk wall with three 10 mm nuts.  Two are very visible, the third nut is buried INSIDE the blower housing.  You need to first disassemble the blower housing by removing four philips screws around the perimeter of the housing.  Once the outer half of the housing is removed, the third 10 mm nut is clearly visible inside the inner half of the housing.||||reg||1
Z||000010||maryjane||10-24-2001||11:07 PM||||Thanks for posting back, Mark. Now others can find it in a search.||||reg||14
